class Solution {
    public void insertBeforeNode(ListNode node, int X) {

        // Create the new node with the given value.
        ListNode newNode = new ListNode(X);

        // The new node should come before the given node,
        // so it points forward to the given node.
        newNode.next = node;

        // The new node should point backward to the node
        // that was previously before the given node.
        newNode.prev = node.prev;

        // The previous node now needs to point forward
        // to the new node instead of directly to node.
        node.prev.next = newNode;

        // The given node now needs to point backward
        // to the new node.
        node.prev = newNode;
    }
}

📖 Book Note

When the node reference is already given, no traversal is needed. Create the new node and insert it between node.prev and node: set newNode.prev = node.prev, newNode.next = node, node.prev.next = newNode, and node.prev = newNode. Since the problem guarantees that the given node is not the head, node.prev is available.

Time: O(1)
Auxiliary Space: O(1)
